---
/*
 * SiteFooter — the shell's footer. Minimal on purpose: a wordmark, two short link columns,
 * one status line, one legal line. No newsletter and no social icons, because there is no
 * newsletter and there are no accounts.
 *
 * EVERY CLAIM AND EVERY LINK WAS CHECKED, logged out, on the day this was written:
 *   github.com/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm            HTTP 200   (public)
 *   .../cloud-swarm/blob/main/LICENSE          HTTP 200   (MIT, © 2026 Yulan Ventures, LLC)
 *   github.com/Ridge-io/coswarm-dist           HTTP 404   -> deliberately NOT linked
 *   controls: a made-up repo and a made-up blob path both returned 404, so the 200s above
 *   are a real signal and not a GitHub soft-200.
 *
 * The releases repo is the one thing here with no link, and that is the honest rendering:
 * it is still private, so a "Releases" link would dead-end. It is not mentioned at all
 * rather than mentioned-and-unclickable, which only raises a question the page cannot answer.
 *
 * INTEGRATION — THIS IS NOW THE ONLY FOOTER ON THE SITE. Three existed, built in parallel:
 * this one, landing/SiteFoot.astro and download/PageFoot.astro, plus the older
 * components/Footer.astro. They disagreed on layout, on link set, and — worse — on fact:
 * two of them stated in rendered markup that the repository is "private during dogfood".
 * Re-checked live on the day of integration: github.com/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm answers 200
 * logged out and .../blob/main/LICENSE answers 200, with a made-up repo path returning 404
 * as the control, so the 200s are real and the "private" claim was false on two pages. The
 * other three footers are deleted; every page now falls through to Base.astro's default slot.
 *
 * "/#authority" was removed from the Product column at the same time. That id no longer
 * exists on the landing page (the framing it named was retired), so it was a dead link on
 * all four pages, and it was one of the two sources of the only retired vocabulary left in
 * the built output.
 *
 * The hash anchor points at a section id that exists on the landing page today, prefixed
 * with "/" so it still resolves from a route that is not "/". The rest are real routes.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE STATUS LINE MUST FOLLOW THE DEPLOYED SIGN-UP GATE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * D-023 RETIRED two successive status lines which said there was no public sign-up. Their
 * exact wording is deliberately not preserved here: even though frontmatter is stripped,
 * quoting a false launch state as if it were current made the next edit easier to get wrong.
 *
 * Self-serve workspace creation exists on the server: the
 * `create_workspace` command is handled by createSelfServeWorkspace() in
 * supabase/functions/command/index.ts, dispatched ahead of route resolution because a new
 * caller has no membership yet. Production now sets `SWARM_SELF_SERVE=1`; sign-up is open.
 *
 * WHAT THE LINE SAYS NOW, AND WHERE EACH PART WAS CHECKED IN THAT FILE:
 *   three workspaces ........ FREE_TIER_WORKSPACE_LIMIT = 3 (:330), compared against a
 *                             count of rows with archived_at IS NULL (:2245-2251). There is
 *                             no user-facing archive/free-a-slot action, so the public line
 *                             states the limit without promising one.
 *   per verified identity ... the handler refuses a non-human credential and then refuses
 *                             an unverified one (:2183-2186); identityVerified is Supabase's
 *                             email_confirmed_at (:3205).
 *   no card ................. there is no billing surface in this repo. Enumerated, not
 *                             assumed: a case-insensitive grep for stripe|billing|payment|
 *                             subscription|invoice|\bprice\b over src/, supabase/functions/,
 *                             supabase/migrations/ and package.json returns 0 hits, and the
 *                             SAME grep for "workspace" over the SAME paths returns 1002 —
 *                             so the zero is a measurement and not a broken instrument.
 *
 * The handler's first gate remains `SWARM_SELF_SERVE === "1"` (:323, :2182), so this status
 * is deployment truth rather than a timeless product property. If the gate changes, update
 * the shared header, this footer, and the rendered shell observer together.
 *
 * THE LEGAL COLUMN AND SIGNUP ASSENT SHARE THESE DESTINATIONS. /terms and /privacy now
 * exist as explicit drafts; /app links the same two paths when a person signs in. Keeping
 * both surfaces on the shared routes prevents policy links from drifting by entry point.
 */

/* `?raw` rather than fs.readFileSync: Vite resolves these at compile time, relative to THIS
 * file. readFileSync + import.meta.url does not work here — by the time the frontmatter runs
 * it lives in dist/.prerender/chunks/, so the relative path pointed at site/src/cloud/. */
import pkgSource from "../../../package.json?raw";
import cloudConfigSource from "../../../src/cloud/config.ts?raw";

import Wordmark from "./Wordmark.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm";

/*
 * THE VERSION LINE IS READ FROM THE BINARY'S OWN SOURCES AT BUILD TIME, NOT TYPED.
 *
 * The footer's left column used to be a wordmark and two lines of blurb against a column
 * that ran 80px further down, so it opened on a hole. The fix is not filler: it is the one
 * fact a footer on a pre-launch developer tool has earned the right to state, and the only
 * honest way to state it is to derive it.
 *
 *   cswarm 0.0.1   <- package.json "version" at the repo root, which is what
 *                     packageVersion() feeds CLI_BUILD_VERSION (src/cli.ts:140)
 *   protocol 0.1.0 <- CLIENT_PROTOCOL_VERSION (src/cloud/config.ts:3)
 *
 * Rendered in the same shape the binary prints for `--version` (src/cli.ts:246 and :1737):
 * `cswarm <v> (protocol <p>)`. Checked against the built CLI on the day this was written:
 *   $ node dist/cli.js --version   ->  coswarm 0.0.1 (protocol 0.1.0)
 *
 * NOTE THE MISMATCH ON THE LINE ABOVE, IT IS DELIBERATE. That is the output as measured, under
 * the old binary name; nobody has re-run `--version` on a renamed build. The literal prefix in
 * `versionLine` below reads `cswarm`, and that was not assumed from the rename — it was read
 * off the two places the binary composes the string, which have MOVED since this note was
 * written: src/cli.ts:248 and :1804 (the note's old :246 and :1737 are stale line numbers for
 * the same two sites). Both now build `cswarm ${CLI_BUILD_VERSION} (protocol
 * ${CLIENT_PROTOCOL_VERSION})`, so the shape below still matches the binary's.
 *
 * Only the prefix is a literal here; both NUMBERS stay derived at build time from
 * package.json and config.ts, and both still throw if they read empty — so the part that
 * could silently go stale did not change.
 *
 * Both reads throw at BUILD time if they come back empty, so this can never silently ship a
 * blank or a stale version. A hard-coded string would have gone stale on the next bump; a
 * build error is cheaper than a footer that lies about which binary is current.
 */
const cliVersion: unknown = JSON.parse(pkgSource).version;

const protocolVersion = cloudConfigSource.match(
  /CLIENT_PROTOCOL_VERSION\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"/
)?.[1];

if (typeof cliVersion !== "string" || !cliVersion) {
  throw new Error(
    "SiteFooter: could not read `version` from the repo-root package.json. The footer states " +
      "the shipping CLI version and must not render a blank or a guess."
  );
}
if (!protocolVersion) {
  throw new Error(
    "SiteFooter: could not read CLIENT_PROTOCOL_VERSION from src/cloud/config.ts. Either the " +
      "constant moved or its shape changed; fix the read rather than hard-coding the number."
  );
}

const versionLine = `cswarm ${cliVersion} (protocol ${protocolVersion})`;

const COLUMNS = [
  {
    title: "Product",
    links: [
      { href: "/#how-it-works", label: "How it works" },
      /* "Install", matching the header nav and the page's own h1 and eyebrow. The route is
       * still /download — URL churn is not worth it — but nothing visible says "download"
       * or "the CLI" any more, so one destination now has one name. */
      { href: "/download", label: "Install" },
      { href: "/app", label: "Dashboard" },
      { href: "/app", label: "Create a workspace" },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Project",
    links: [
      { href: REPO, label: "GitHub", external: true },
      { href: `${REPO}/blob/main/LICENSE`, label: "MIT license", external: true },
    ],
  },
  /* Legal. Added when the three documents landed, EXTENDING this list rather than forking
   * it — the note at the top of this file records what forking the footer cost last time.
   *
   * A third column and not a row of small print in .ft__bar: the bottom bar is already a
   * status sentence plus the copyright line, and three more links there would push the
   * legal line to a second row at every width below ~72rem. It also puts these where a
   * reader looks for them, which is a footer column headed "Legal".
   *
   * These are real routes — site/src/pages/terms.astro, privacy.astro and
   * acceptable-use.astro — not hashes, so they resolve from all seven routes. Every one of
   * them renders a draft banner today; they are written and they are not yet in force.
   *
   * The "MIT license" link one column left is about the SOURCE CODE. /terms governs the
   * HOSTED SERVICE, and says so out loud in its "Our software and your licence to use it"
   * section, because a reader who finds MIT first will otherwise assume it covers both. */
  {
    title: "Legal",
    links: [
      { href: "/terms", label: "Terms of Service" },
      { href: "/privacy", label: "Privacy" },
      { href: "/acceptable-use", label: "Acceptable use" },
    ],
  },
];

/* Built at build time. The site is static, so this is the year of the last deploy — which is
 * the only year a static page can honestly claim. */
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---

<footer class="ft">
  <div class="page ft__inner">
    <div class="ft__top">
      <div class="ft__brand">
        <Wordmark href="/" markSize={20} />
        <p class="ft__blurb">
          Every agent says what it is working on, so the next one finds out before it
          starts the same job.
        </p>
        <p class="ft__version">
          <span class="ft__version-label">Shipping</span>
          <span class="mono ft__version-str">{versionLine}</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ft__cols">
        {
          COLUMNS.map((col) => (
            <nav class="ft__col" aria-label={col.title}>
              <h2 class="ft__h">{col.title}</h2>
              <ul class="ft__list">
                {col.links.map((link) => (
                  <li>
                    <a
                      class="ft__link"
                      href={link.href}
                      target={link.external ? "_blank" : undefined}
                      rel={link.external ? "noopener noreferrer" : undefined}
                    >
                      {link.label}
                      {link.external && (
                        <span class="ft__sr"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
                      )}
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </nav>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ft__bar">
      <p class="ft__status">
        <span class="ft__pip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>
          <strong>Signup is open.</strong> Free — three workspaces, no card.
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="ft__legal">
        © {year} Yulan Ventures, LLC · MIT · no tracking on this page
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>

<style>
  /* NO margin-top. This footer had 6rem of it, which was invisible while it rendered on
     nothing. Now that it is the site’s only footer it lands under a section that already
     ends on a full --pad-section-tight (86.4px at 1440), and the two stack: measured 182px
     of empty page above the rule. Both of the per-page footers that this one replaced had
     independently discovered and fixed the same thing — landing/SiteFoot called it "a ~220px
     band of nothing", download/PageFoot "a ~225px empty band". The border and the change of
     background are what separate the footer; it does not also need a gap. */
  .ft {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--elev-1);
  }

  /* Was --s-16 (64px) on top. Measured at 1440 the footer opened on a hole: the brand
     column ended 83px above the link column and the divider sat another 81px below that,
     so the block was large and half empty. The top pad comes down one step and the rest of
     the gap is closed by .ft__bar's margin, below. */
  .ft__inner {
    padding-block: var(--s-12) var(--s-8);
  }

  .ft__top {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: start;
    gap: var(--s-12);
  }

  /* --- brand --------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .ft__brand {
    /* Holds the blurb to a readable measure without letting it starve the columns
       when the row is tight. */
    flex: 1 1 20rem;
    max-width: 30rem;
  }

  /* The .ft__word / .ft__mark rules are gone: the wordmark is Wordmark.astro now. They set
     the word at --t-base / -0.024em / 0.5rem gap while both header copies set
     1.0625rem / -0.028em / 0.55rem, so the same mark was typeset two ways on one page. */

  .ft__blurb {
    margin-top: var(--s-3);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 38ch;
  }

  /* The version line. It is the reason this column no longer bottoms out 80px above the one
     beside it, and it is a real string read out of the binary's own sources at build time —
     see the note in the frontmatter. Set in mono because that is how it is printed. */
  .ft__version {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-2) var(--s-3);
    margin-top: var(--s-5);
  }

  .ft__version-label {
    font-size: var(--t-2xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-2xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* Same reasoning as .ft__h: --text-faint is 4.09:1 on --elev-1 and is not allowed to
       carry text this small. --text-muted is 7.38:1 here. */
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .ft__version-str {
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-xs);
    color: var(--text);
    /* Tabular figures so a version bump cannot reflow the line. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* --- columns ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .ft__cols {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-16);
  }

  .ft__col {
    min-width: 8rem;
  }

  .ft__h {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-4);
    font-size: var(--t-2xs);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--lh-2xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* NOT --text-faint. It measures 4.09:1 on this footer's --elev-1 surface, under the 4.5
       AA needs, and tokens.css says outright that it is for large text and non-text only.
       --text-muted is 7.38:1 here. The same applies to .ft__legal below. */
    color: var(--text-muted);
    /* Uppercase tracking leaves a gap on the right; pull it back so the label optically
       aligns with the links under it. */
    margin-right: calc(var(--track-eyebrow) * -1);
  }

  .ft__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .ft__link {
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    transition: color var(--dur-1) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .ft__link:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .ft__sr {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* --- bottom bar ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .ft__bar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    /* Was --s-16 (64px). The rule sat 81px under the taller column with nothing in between,
       which read as the footer running out rather than closing. --s-6 puts it a card's
       padding below the last link — close enough to belong to the block above it. */
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
    padding-top: var(--s-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .ft__status {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    max-width: 54ch;
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .ft__status strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* Amber, not green: this is "not finished yet", not "all good". The dot is decorative —
     the sentence beside it carries the whole meaning, so nothing is lost without colour. */
  .ft__pip {
    flex: none;
    inline-size: 6px;
    block-size: 6px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--warning);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in oklab, var(--warning) 15%, transparent);
    /* Baseline alignment puts a 6px box on the text baseline; nudge it onto the x-height. */
    transform: translateY(-0.15em);
  }

  .ft__legal {
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* --- responsive ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .ft__top {
      gap: var(--s-10);
    }

    .ft__cols {
      gap: var(--s-12);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .ft {
      margin-top: var(--s-16);
    }

    .ft__inner {
      padding-block: var(--s-12) var(--s-8);
    }

    .ft__brand,
    .ft__blurb {
      max-width: none;
    }

    .ft__cols {
      /* Two columns rather than a stack: both are short, and stacking them pushes the
         legal line a whole screen away from the links it belongs with. */
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-8);
      width: 100%;
    }

    .ft__bar {
      margin-top: var(--s-8);
    }

    .ft__legal {
      white-space: normal;
    }
  }
</style>
